What grows, and what it costs to grow it
Five stacked layers, read top to bottom. Entry, where any node can take the
role and adding one adds a place to arrive. Serving, where one node holds one
tenant's writes. Routing, where a durable assignment says which node serves
which tenant, with no broker. Work, where runners ask for work and report
back, so adding one adds throughput and nothing else. Data, a database per
tenant, where the unit that moves is a whole tenant. The serving and routing
layers are drawn faint and dashed because they are specified and have no
implementation; the others are built.
